lack's lead stayed in double digits. Opus 5.5 averaged 3.9 points lost per move against the 12k's 2.6, and resigned on move 44.", "moments": [{"turn": 11, "text": "The 12k played E8 here, which lost 6.3 points and moved Black's lead from -0.1 to -6.4. The engine preferred F8. It was the first time the game tipped clearly to White."}, {"turn": 12, "text": "Opus 5.5 answered with D8, which lost 6.0 points and put Black's lead back at -0.3. The engine's choice was F6. The 6.4-point lead from the 12k's slip lasted exactly one move."}, {"turn": 24, "text": "The 12k's C2 had just swung Black's lead from +6.1 to -6.6, but Opus 5.5's B2 lost 12.3 points and put it back at +5.8. The engine wanted G2. It was a second chance given and returned within one move."}, {"turn": 26, "text": "After the 12k's D1 (18.2 points lost) left Black's lead at -12.4, Opus 5.5 played H4, the worst move of the game at 24.8 points lost. Black's lead jumped to +12.3 and the engine's choice had been G2. Black stayed ahead for the rest of the game."}, {"turn": 40, "text": "Opus 5.5 played J2 and lost 7.6 points, taking Black's lead from +18.6 to +26.2 against the engine's A2. By now the lead was already large. It reached +30.3 before Opus 5.5 resigned."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "after.py plays one move the player names, removes captures and prints group liberties, while count.py counts stones and territory and libs.py prints liberties on the current board. None of them loops over candidate moves, scores moves, or looks ahead past the single named move, so they only inspect the position.", "stop": false}}